# TODO

1. A switch for plan to read every revision from its header, passing over the CHANGELOGs build wrote, so that planning again from the headers wants only revisions.yml removed rather than every CHANGELOG with it.
2. Take the binary's name from the CHANGELOG, rather than assuming the sole entry in bin/.
3. Produce a single CHANGELOG in the last version directory, rather than a cumulative one in every directory.
4. Work for git repositories, whether by writing outside the repository or by rewriting history.
5. Take the entry's date from the latest header in the tree, not only the main file's. SimpleCSV's second `0.9.3` changed only `String/split_csv.rb`, whose header says 20100612, and was dated 20100526 from the library header. When this is done, the snapshot tier having been as of 0.19.0, the imported histories can have their narration regenerated in one pass: `dsv` needs a re-import through `_github_staging/SimpleCSV/synthetic/` and a force-push, so it is done once, before any consumer points at the repository.
6. Multiple dates in a CHANGELOG heading, as a header's date line already carries them: `## 20260913, 1005`, escalating against the element before it, the last being the completion date.  It fails quietly: `Changelog::Format::DatedHeading`'s `HEADING` and `BOUNDARY` want eight digits exactly, so such a heading begins no entry of its own.  Below the newest, the heading is read as an unversioned entry and the entries beneath it take the date of the heading above; as the newest, it and its entries go with the file's title.  `expand_dates` already reads the escalating form for a header's date line and would serve, the entry taking the last of the dates; and `write_changelog` writes the single `entry[:date]` it holds, so it would have to keep the heading it read rather than reduce it.  Nothing needs this: changelogger writes a single date by design, reducing a multi-date header to its completion date, so only a hand-written CHANGELOG would carry one.  Raised upon dsv 0.12.4, whose work spanned 20260913 and 1005; the span went into the entry's prose instead, which the reader passes through untouched.
